    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d106">

                <pb facs="0260" n="142" sameAs="frus:frus1889/pg142"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Tsui Kwo Yin to
                        Mr. Blaine.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Chinese Legation</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1889-10-16"><hi rendition="#italic">October</hi> 16, 1889</date>.
                        (Received October 19.)</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have received information, not of an
                    official character, that some new measures of your excellency’s Government have
                    been adopted which it is reported are working hardship to the subjects of China,
                    who seek to exercise the privilege guarantied to them by treaty stipulations of
                    transit through the territory of the United States.</p>
                <p>Having resort to the provision contained in Article IV of the treaty of 1880
                    between our Governments, I beg of you to be so kind as to inform me at your
                    earliest convenience whether any new legislative measures have been adopted by
                    your Government in relation to the transit of Chinese subjects, and if so what
                    is the character of those measures.</p>
                <p>I improve, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Tsui Kwo Yin.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
